description:
The MSVC debug heap prints leaks like this when a Debug build exits (lesson 4.05):

```text
Detected memory leaks!
Dumping objects ->
C:\src\level.cpp(14) : {157} normal block at 0x000001C4B2E8F0A0, 40 bytes long.
 Data: <                > CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD CD
{156} normal block at 0x000001C4B2E91230, 8 bytes long.
 Data: <*       > 2A 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
Object dump complete.
```

Turn such a report into data a tool can sort and summarise:

```cpp
struct Leak
{
    int allocation;
    std::uint64_t address;
    std::size_t size;
    std::string file;      // empty when the report has no file
    int line;              // 0 when there is no file
    std::vector<std::uint8_t> data;
};

std::vector<Leak> parseLeakReport(const std::string& report);
std::size_t totalLeaked(const std::vector<Leak>& leaks);
std::vector<int> neverWritten(const std::vector<Leak>& leaks);
```

- **parseLeakReport** finds every block line: an optional `file(line) : ` part, then `{number} normal block at 0xADDRESS, SIZE bytes long.` The data line that follows it gives the first bytes as hex pairs after the `>`. Lines that are not block lines or data lines are ignored. Leaks are returned in the order they appear.
- **totalLeaked** adds up the sizes.
- **neverWritten** lists the allocation numbers of leaks whose shown data bytes are all `0xCD` (memory that was allocated and never written), in increasing order.

